## ONNX models and datasets that correspond to the output directories.
## This is the ONNX counterpart of `models_datasets`, used by `run-experiments2.sh` / `run1-2.sh`.

## It expects psi files to be located at the common directory of the variants (e.g. explanations/heart_attack)
#? do not make this assumption but make it explicit

## Unlike `.nnet` files, ONNX carries NO per-feature input domain, and Network2 then silently falls
## back to [0,1] for every feature -- which produces misleadingly weak explanations. Therefore every
## entry here must also provide INPUT_MINS/INPUT_MAXS (comma-separated, one value per input feature,
## in the model's input order). Leave an entry empty ('') only if [0,1] really is the right domain.
## See the "explain-onnx" section of README.md.

## MNIST pixels are stored raw (0-255) and flat (784 columns) in the CSV, so the bounds are the
## full pixel range for every feature. The model's ONNX input is image-shaped ([1,1,28,28]); the
## flat CSV columns land on it in row-major order, which is the order the CSV already uses.

MNIST_PIXEL_MIN=$(printf '0,%.0s' $(seq 1 783))0
MNIST_PIXEL_MAX=$(printf '255,%.0s' $(seq 1 783))255

## The CNN family under models/mnist/cnn/unit/ is exported WITHOUT the 1/255 folding, so it
## expects already-scaled pixels and pairs with datasets/mnist/mnist_s100_scaled.csv ([0,1]).
## The unfolded (raw 0-255) copies in models/mnist/cnn/ pair with mnist_quick/mnist_short instead;
## crossing the two silently produces garbage predictions, not an error.

MNIST_UNIT_MIN=$(printf '0,%.0s' $(seq 1 783))0
MNIST_UNIT_MAX=$(printf '1,%.0s' $(seq 1 783))1

